readings.c1s1:
“George!” was the ''war-cry'' of England, “Montjoie Saint Denis!” the ''war-cry'' of France.

{{smaller block|<poem>Faithful to his noble vow, his ''war-cry'' filled the air;
“Be honour’d aye the bravest knight, beloved the fairest fair.”</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Romance of Dunois (trans.).}}}}

'''ward'''<sup>1</sup> (wârd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ward'', < AS. ''weard'', m., a keeper, watchman, guard, guardian, = OS. ''ward'' = OHG. MHG. G. ''wart'' (in comp.) = Icel. ''vörthr'' (''varth-''), m., a watchman, a watch, = Goth. *''wards'', in comp. ''daura-wards'', m., doorkeeper; also OHG. ''warto'', MHG. ''warte'' = Goth. ''wardja'', m., keeper, watchman; also OHG. ''warta'' = Goth. ''wardō'', f., in comp. ''daura-wardō'', a keeper; with formative ''-d'', from the root *''war'' in ''ware'', ''wary'', etc.: see ''ware''<sup>1</sup>, ''wear''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''ward''<sup>2</sup>, and see ''ward''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', which is derived from both ''ward''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', and ''ward''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.'' Hence, in comp., ''bearward'', ''gateward'', ''hayward'', ''steward'' (''styward''), ''woodward'', etc.] A keeper; watchman; warden. [Archaic.]

{{smaller block|<poem>And with that breth helle brake with alle Beliales barres;
For eny wye other ''warde'' wyde openede the gates.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xxi. 368.}}}}

{{smaller|'''City ward†.''' See ''city''.}}

'''ward'''<sup>1</sup> (wârd), ''v.'' [< ME. ''warden'', ''wardien'', < AS. ''weardian'', keep, watch, hold, possess (= OS. ''wardōn'' = OFries. ''wardia'' = MLG. ''warden'' = OHG. MHG. G. ''warten'', watch, = Icel. ''vartha'', warrant, etc.), < ''weard'', m., keeper, ''weard'', f., keeping: see ''ward''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', ''ward''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.'' Hence (from MHG. ''warten'') OF. ''warder'', ''guarder'', ''garder'' = Pr. ''gardar'', ''guardar'' = Sp. Pg. ''guardar'' = It. ''guardare'', watch, guard: see ''guard'', ''v.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To take care of; keep in safety; watch;

readings.c1s2:
guard; defend; protect.
{{smaller block|<poem>God me ''ward'' and kepe fro werk diabolike,
And stedfaste me hold in feith Catholike!</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3490.}}
<poem>Tell him it was a hand that ''warded'' him
From thousand dangers.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., iii. 1. 195.}}
Coueting to draw nigh your ships, which if they shal finde not wel watched, or ''warded'', they wil assault.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 229.}}}}
'''2.''' To put under guard; imprison.
{{smaller block|Into which prison were these Christians put, and fast ''warded'' all the winter season.
{{right|''Munday'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 204).}}}}
'''3.''' To fend off; repel; turn aside: commonly followed by ''off''.
{{smaller block|When all is done, there is no ''warding'' the Blows of Fortune. {{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 152.}}
To ''ward off'' the gripe of poverty, you must pretend to be a stranger to her. {{right|''Goldsmith'', The Bee, No. 3.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To keep guard; watch.
{{smaller block|The valiant Captaine Francesco Bagone ''warded'' at the Keepe. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 123.}}}}
'''2.''' To act on the defensive with a weapon; guard one’s self.
{{smaller block|Zelmane, redoubling her blows, drave the stranger to no other shift than to ''ward'' and go back.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}
Halfe their times and labours are spent in watching and ''warding'', onely to defend, but altogether vnable to suppresse the Saluages. {{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, II. 79.}}}}
'''3†.''' To take care: followed by a clause beginning with ''that''.

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>I now of all good here schal fynd by grace;
But ''warde that'' ye be a Monday in thys place.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 805.}}}}

'''ward<sup>2</sup>''' (wârd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ward'', ''warde'', < AS. ''weard'', f., keeping, watch, guard, district, ward, = MLG. ''warde'' = OHG. ''warta'', MHG. ''warte'', ''wart'', f., keeping, watch, guard; an abstract fem. noun, with formative ''-d'', from the root *''war'' in ''ware'', ''wary'', etc.: see ''ware''<sup>1</sup>, ''wear''<sup>2</sup>. From the Teut. are ult., through OF., E. ''guard'', ''n.'' and ''v.'', ''regard'', ''reward'', ''guardian'', ''warden''<sup>1</sup>, etc. Cf. ''ward''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', and ''ward''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', which involves both nouns.] '''1.''' The act of keeping guard; a position or state of watchfulness against surprise, danger, or harm; guard; watch: as, to keep watch and ''ward''. See ''watch''.
{{smaller block|<poem>But I which spend the darke and dreadful night
In watch and ''ward''.</poem>
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Philomene (Steele Glas, etc., ed. Arber, p. 87).}}}}
'''2†.''' A body of persons whose duty it is to guard, protect, or defend; the watch; a defensive force; garrison.
{{smaller block|<poem>Th’ assieged Castles ''ward''
Their stedfast stonds did mightily maintaine.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. xi. 15.}}
<poem>Was frequent heard the changing guard,
And watchword from the sleepless ''ward''.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., iii. 30.}}}}
'''3.''' Means of guarding; defense; protection; preservation.
{{smaller block|The best ''ward'' of mine honour is rewarding my dependents.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iii. 1. 133.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>I think I have a close ''ward'', and a sure one—
An honest mind.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, iii. 2.}}}}

'''4†.''' The outworks of a castle.
{{smaller block|<poem>And alle the towres of crystalle schene,
And the ''wardes'' enamelde and overgylt clene.</poem>
{{right|''Hampole.'' (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

'''5.''' A guarded or defensive motion or position in fencing, or the like; a turning aside or intercepting of a blow, thrust, etc.
{{smaller block|<poem>1 ''Scholler.'' Ah, well thrust!
2 ''Scholler.'' But mark the ''ward''.</poem>
{{right|''Greene'', Friar Bacon and Friar Bungay.}}
Thou knowest my old ''ward''; here I lay, and thus I bore my point.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 4. 215.}}}}

'''6.''' The state of being under a guard; confinement under a guard, warder, or keeper; custody; confinement; jail.
{{smaller block|He would be punished and committed to ''ward''.
{{right|''Latimer'', 2d Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}
He put them in ''ward'' in the house of the captain of the guard.
{{right|Gen. xl. 3.}}}}

'''7.''' Guardianship; control or care of a minor.
{{smaller block|Item, my Lord of Hungerford has writen to me for to have the ''warde'' of Robert Monpyns[on]is sone, wher of I am agreed that he schal (have) hit like as I has wretyn to hym in a letter, of the whech I send zow a cope closed here in.
{{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 94.}}
It is inconvenient in Ireland that the ''wards'' and marriages of gentlemen’s children should be in the disposal of any of those lords.
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}

'''8.''' The state of being under the care, control, or protection of a guardian; the condition of being under guardianship.
{{smaller block|I must attend his majesty’s command, to whom I am now in ''ward''.
{{right|''Shak.'', All’s Well, i. 1. 5.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|The decay of estates in ''ward'' by the abuse of the powers of wardship. {{right|''R. W. Dixon'', Hist. Church of Eng., ii.}}}}

'''9.''' One who or that which is guarded; specifically, a minor or person under guardianship. {{smaller|(''a'') In ''feudal law'', the heir of the king’s tenant in capite, during his nonage. (''b'') In ''British law'', a minor under the protection of the Court of Chancery, generally called a ''ward in Chancery'', or a ''ward of court.'' To marry a ward of court without consent of the court is a contempt. The court has power, if the ward has property, to appoint a guardian, if there is none, and to supervise his administration, and remove him.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>My lord, he ’s a great ''ward'', wealthy, but simple;
His parts consist in acres.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton'', Women Beware Women, iii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') In ''U. S. law'', a minor for whom a guardian is appointed.}}

'''10.''' A division. {{smaller|(''a'') A band or company.}}
{{smaller block|Habshabiah, Sherebiah, and Jeshua the son of Kadmiel, with their brethren over against them, to praise and to give thanks, according to the commandment of David the man of God, ''ward'' over against ''ward''. {{right|Neh. xii. 24.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b''†) A division of an army; a brigade, battalion, or regiment.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The kyng of Lybie, callid Lamadone,
The ix<sup>te</sup> ''warde'' hadde att his leding.</poem>
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2172.}}
The thirde ''warde'' lede the kynge Boors of Gannes, that full wele cowde hem guyde, and were in his company iiij<sup>ml</sup> men wele horsed. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 151.}}
Somerset, expecting to have been followed by Lord Wenlock, who commanded what was called “the middle ''ward''” of that army, allowed himself to be lured into a pursuit. {{right|''J. Gairdner'', Richard III., i.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') A certain division, section, or quarter of a town or city, such as is under the charge of an alderman, or as is constituted for the convenient transaction of local public business through committees appointed by the inhabitants, or merely for the purposes of elections.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Throughout the trembling city placed a guard,
Dealing an equal share to every ''ward''. {{right|''Dryden.''}}</poem>}}
{{smaller|(''d'') A territorial division of some counties in Great Britain, as Lanarkshire and Renfrewshire in Scotland, and Northumberland and Cumberland in the north of England. (''e'') The division of a forest. (''f'') One of the apartments into which a hospital is divided: as, a fever ''ward;'' a convalescent ''ward.''}}

'''11.''' A curved ridge of metal inside a lock, forming an obstacle to the passage of a key which has not a corresponding notch; also, the notch or slot in the web or bit of a key into which such a ridge fits when the key is applied.
{{smaller block|The wards of a lock are often named according to their shapes: as, L-''ward;'' T-''ward.'' The wards are usually made of sheet-metal bent into a round form, and hence are sometimes termed ''wheels.'' See cut under ''pick''<sup>1</sup>, 4.
<poem>A key
That winds through secret ''wards.''</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Memory.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Casual, casualty, condemned ward.''' See the qualifying words.—'''Casualty of wards.''' See ''casualty.''—'''Isolating ward,''' a room in a hospital set apart for the reception of patients suffering with contagious disease, or who must for any cause be kept from contact with others in the hospital.—'''Police-jury ward,''' in Louisiana, the chief subdivision of the parish.—'''Watch and ward.''' See ''watch.''}}

'''ward'''<sup>3</sup>†, ''adv.'' [< ME. ''ward'', a quasi-adverb, being the suffix ''-ward'' separated from its base, as in ''to'' me ''ward''. See ''-ward'' and ''toward''.] The suffix ''-ward'' separated as a distinct word.

'''-ward''' (wärd). [< ME. ''-ward'', < AS. ''-weard'' = OS. ''-ward'' = OFries. ''-ward'' = D. ''-waart'' = MLG

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The Countess asked to be shown some of the prisoners’ soup. The ''warden'' brought some to her in a clean fresh plate.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXVII. 509.}}}}
'''3.''' The title given to the head of some colleges and schools, and to the superior of some conventual churches.
{{smaller block|<poem>Our corn is stoln, men wil us fooles calle,
Bathe the ''wardeyn'' and oure felawes alle.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 192.}}}}
{{smaller block|And all way the ''Wardeyne'' of the seyd ffrers or sum of hys Brothern by hys assignment Daly accompanyd with vs Informyng And shewing vnto vs the holy places with in the holy lande. ''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 26.}}
'''4.''' In Connecticut boroughs, the chief executive officer of the municipal government; in a few Rhode Island towns, a judicial officer. In colonial times the name was sometimes used in place of ''fire-warden'' or ''fire-ward''.—{{smaller|'''Port warden,''' an officer invested with the chief authority in a port.—'''Warden of a church.''' See ''churchwarden''.—'''Warden of a university,''' the master or president of a university.—'''Warden of the Cinque Ports,''' the governor of the havens called the Cinque Ports, and their dependencies, who has the authority of an admiral, and has power to hold a court of admiralty and courts of law and equity. See ''Cinque Ports'', under ''cinque''.—'''Warden of the marches.''' See ''march''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Warden of the mint.''' See ''mint''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Warden of the stews,''' a town officer, one of several mentioned in the fifteenth century: apparently one who had charge of pens for cattle, hogs, etc., perhaps a pound. Compare ''hog-mace''.}}

'''warden<sup>2</sup>''' (wâr′dn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wardun'', ''wardone''; usually associated with ''warden''<sup>1</sup>, and taken to mean a pear that may be kept long (cf. OF. ''poire de garde'', “a warden, or winter peare, a peare which may be kept verie long,” Cotgrave):
